// result_end

> Results

```
=== ZeroR on the play-tennis dataset ===
class counts:  no=5  yes=9  (n=14)
ZeroR always predicts: yes
baseline accuracy: 0.642857  (= 9/14)
prediction for any input, e.g. a rainy day: yes
```

Nine of fourteen days are "yes", so ZeroR predicts "yes" for everything and scores
\(9/14\approx 64\%\) \-\- with no features used at all. Any real classifier on this
data must clear that bar to justify its complexity; the companion OneR report shows
a single-attribute rule already does.


> Why it matters

ZeroR is trivial by design, and that is the point: it calibrates expectations. On
*imbalanced* data its accuracy can be very high (99% if 99% of cases are negative),
which is exactly why accuracy alone is a misleading metric there \-\- ZeroR "achieves"
99% while detecting none of the positives. Reporting the ZeroR baseline (and
preferring metrics like balanced accuracy, F1, or AUC when classes are skewed) is
basic hygiene in any classification study.

> Complexity

One pass to tally class counts: \(O(n)\) time, \(O(k)\) memory for \(k\) classes.
Prediction is \(O(1)\).
